In article <14838@uqcspe.cs.uq.oz.au> warwick@cs.uq.oz.au writes:
>
>_Balance_ is an important aspect of any game.  A game is not fun if it
>only involves going through motions and no risk or suprise occurs.
>
>Below are some of the things I believe are damaging the game balance in NH.
>I am not advocating that they be removed from the game, just pointing out
>that they can upset balance.
>
>	1. Resistance.  As in fire resistance, etc.  These effects means that
>		the game gets EASIER as the player progresses.

Well, this can be countered by making the game more challenging on the
lower levels.  You could almost as easily argue that increasing hit
points with level makes the game easier as it progresses, and I can't
imagine wanting to do away with _that_.  Just view resistances as
additions to character power (like increased hit points and better
armor/weapons) that allow the player to deal with greater and greater
challenges. 
>	2. Monsters based on player level.  This discourages a player from
>		increasing level (eg. the nurse stuff I've heard of).  It
>		also distorts the realism of the game.
I agree.  I could never figure out a rationale for this.
>	3. Wishing for powerful objects.  Quest-type objects can be wished
>		for.  This devalues the quest, and forces other parts of
>		the game to distort.
>
>My suggestions for rebalancing these effects are:
>
>
>        1. All resistances/outside-gained-intrinsics wear off, or 
>                take player action/inaction to maintain (eg.  ESP lost
>                by homicide/elvicide).  This drives the player to
>                maintain resistances.  
I'm not sure I like this.  There's something "exciting" about getting
a relatively permanent intrinsic.  This doesn't mean intrinsics can't
be lost for a _reason_ (like ESP currently is), but I don't think they
should just wear out.

If they're really too powerful, here's an idea:  Make intrinsic
resistances numbers, say from 1 to 5.  You can increase an intrinsic
by doing the same thing you do to gain it, but the higher the value
already is, the harder it is to increase it further (if your
resistance is currently n, maybe (1/(n+1)) times normal chance).

Now, attacks could have a particular strength (which would translate
directly into how much damage is done).  For example (feel free to
quibble with my numbers):

1:  1 HP      (Grid Bugs)
2:  1-8 HP    (Fire Ants)    
3:  5-20 HP   (Molds)
4:  10-50 HP  (Dragons)
5:  30-100 HP (Really Nasty Things [tm], maybe falling in lava pools?)

Now, if your resistance is higher or equal to the damage type, you
take no damage.  If it's lower, you take damage in a category equal to
the distance (so if a Dragon breathes on you, you take 10-50 HP if you
have no relevant resistance, 5-20 if you have resistance 1, 1-8 if you
have resistance 2, 1 HP only if you have resistance 3, and no damage
if you have 4 or 5).

Rings of X resistance could be +1, +2, or (very rarely) +3.

Gehennom might cause type 3 damage every turn.  Living there very long
with anything less than level 2 Fire resistance would probably be
impossible;  doing it with level 2 might be doable, but it would be a
big pain.  3 or higher would make it feel like home.

This would make it harder to be completely impervious to attacks while
still allowing people to get some semi-permanent protection for their
characters. 

>	2. Have low level monsters less likely to carry powerful objects
>		(reducing gain in staying on lower levels).  This drives
>		the player downward.  (have monster level based on dungeon
>		level).
Sounds good.  Another idea:  Have levels start to "run out" of
monsters:  Make the generation slow down after a while;  maybe even
eventually stop.  Eventually, hunger will kick in and push people
onward.
 
>	3. Have different wishpower for different objects.  The WoW in
>		the castle would be powerful, but those found randomly
>                generally weak (you wish for 3 potions of extra healing).
>		Wishes from genies and water trolls be based on the player
>		level [reasoning:  the wish granter will not grant a
>		powerful wish to some wimp they could bash, but would
>		be more fearsome and submissive to powerful players] 

Maybe...adding a general wish penalty (as I suggested a couple of
weeks ago) might also work.
IMHO, quest items (like PYEC) should _not_ be wishable for at all.
"That will not be done, Mortal."
Wishes _should_ be powerful, though (if costly).  If there are too
many WoW, reduce the number.



